  political_jpdiet:
    "IMPORTANT: This is a FICTIONAL Japanese Diet Member campaign poster — NOT a magazine cover. Transform the person in this photo into a FICTIONAL Japanese national Diet (衆議院議員) candidate in the iconic Japanese street-campaign-poster aesthetic. Keep their face recognizable. Style as an energetic campaigning Japanese politician — clean dark suit with white shirt and bold tie, the signature white 襷 (tasuki) sash worn diagonally across the torso with red Japanese text (the candidate's name in red kanji on white sash), energetic confident fist-pump or thumbs-up campaign pose, sincere determined smile, bright solid-color campaign poster background (often solid red, royal blue, or yellow), professional studio campaign lighting. Japanese Diet campaign poster layout: candidate's full Japanese name in MASSIVE brush-stroke or bold sans-serif kanji typography occupying most of the poster (often vertically stacked, big and impactful), fictional party emblem placeholder, the campaign slogan in vertical Japanese (e.g., '日本を変える' / '未来へ' / '声を聞く' / '勝負の時'), district name, '衆議院議員候補' (Diet member candidate) text label, fictional candidate number in a circle. Iconic Japanese street Diet election poster layout.",
  political_jpcity:
    "IMPORTANT: This is a FICTIONAL Japanese City/Prefecture Council candidate poster — NOT a magazine cover. Transform the person in this photo into a FICTIONAL Japanese local council member (市議会議員 or 県議会議員) candidate in the friendly local-Japanese-politician poster aesthetic. Keep their face recognizable. Style as an approachable local Japanese politician — clean smart-casual outfit (suit jacket over open-collar shirt, or polo shirt for warmer everyman feel), warm friendly genuine smile with direct eye contact, slight friendly head tilt or open hand gesture, bright cheerful solid-color campaign poster background (warm yellow, light sky blue, or pink), approachable bright lighting. Local Japanese election poster layout: candidate's full name in HUGE bold kanji calligraphy or modern sans-serif typography (most prominent element on the poster), simple friendly local slogan in Japanese (e.g., '地域のために' / '私たちの街' / '安心の暮らし' / '若い力で改革を'), district/city/prefecture name prominent in smaller text, '市議会議員候補' or '県議会議員候補' label, fictional candidate number circle, sometimes a list of 3-4 short policy bullet points in Japanese (e.g., '子育て支援、高齢者福祉、地域活性化'). Japanese local council election poster layout, approachable and community-friendly.",
